# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-15 (M-struct-passing, the step that landed `f32`).
# Symptom: `to_i64` on an `f32` returned `ok(0)` for every input — `to_i64(7.9)`
# was `0`, and `to_i64` of a value far past `i64`'s range reported `ok` rather
# than the failure it owes. Exit 0, no diagnostic anywhere, and the only visible
# trace was a `-Wunused-but-set-variable` on a temporary in the generated C.
#
# Cause: the type checker was widened to accept either float width and
# `emit/convert.rs`'s arm was left at `Ty::Float(FloatKind::F64)`, so an `f32`
# source fell to a `_ => return` that emitted **nothing**. The destination is a
# `T?` and panel 021's rule zero-initialises every refcounted slot, so the caller
# read `tag = 0` — which is the `ok` tag — carrying a zero payload.
#
# Two guards that could not see it, and both for stated reasons:
# `-Werror=uninitialized` is blind because the zero-initialiser is deliberate,
# and the rustc rename that turned `Ty::F64` into `Ty::Float(FloatKind)` could
# not reach an arm whose sibling was already a catch-all. That is CLAUDE.md §11's
# loud-fallback rule paying for itself a third time: the `_ => return` is now
# `unreachable!` naming what depends on it.
#
# The case asserts the two answers the defect got wrong — a truncation and a
# refusal — because either alone passes under a stuck zero.

function main()
    small: f32 @ 7.9
    print(to_i64(small).must())
    negative: f32 @ -7.9
    print(to_i64(negative).must())
    huge: f32 @ 100000000000000000000000000000.0
    print(to_i64(huge).is_err())
    # The control: the same conversion at the width that always worked, so a
    # regression that breaks both is told apart from one that breaks this width.
    wide: f64 @ 7.9
    print(to_i64(wide).must())
